<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#indo-european-languages">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indo-European languages</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indo-European languages are a family of related languages spoken across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and much of western and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, descended from a reconstructed ancestor, Proto-Indo-European, probably spoken in the 4th millennium BCE. Its branches include Germanic, Romance, Celtic, Slavic, Greek, Armenian, Iranian and Indo-Aryan, together with extinct groups such as Anatolian (Hittite) and Tocharian. The family was identified in the late 18th and 19th centuries by comparing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indo-european-languages.jpg" alt="Indo-European languages" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bill Williams · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indo-European_Language_Family_Branches_in_Eurasia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Indo-European family is native to the north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and Greater Iran, with further branches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and parts of the southern subcontinent. Historically its languages were also spoken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Eight branches are still spoken: four by hundreds of millions of native speakers each (Indo-Iranian, Italic, Germanic and Balto-Slavic) and four by fewer than fifteen million each (Hellenic, Albanoid, Armenian and Celtic). Two major branches, Anatolian and Tocharian, are extinct.</p><p>Over 3.4 billion people speak an Indo-European language as a first language, the most of any language family. Ethnologue estimates about 446 living languages, of which 313 belong to the Indo-Iranian branch. Several European languages, among them English, French, Portuguese, Russian, Spanish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, spread through colonialism in the modern period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>All Indo-European languages descend from a single prehistoric language, reconstructed as Proto-Indo-European and spoken sometime in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> or early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. Its homeland is the subject of competing hypotheses. The academic consensus supports the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurgan">Kurgan</a> hypothesis, which places it on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic–Caspian steppe</a>, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, in association with the Yamnaya culture.</p><p>The oldest records are isolated Hittite words and names in Assyrian texts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanesh">Kültepe</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, dating to the 20th century BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anitta">Anitta</a> text, in Hittite, dated to 1700 BCE, is the oldest known text in an Indo-European language, and fragmentary Mycenaean Greek records date from between 1450 and 1350 BCE. How the parent language split into its branches is not attested, and the timeline is disputed: David Anthony places Pre-Anatolian at about 4200 BCE and Proto-Indo-Iranian at about 2200 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>European visitors to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> began to notice similarities among Indo-Aryan, Iranian and European languages in the 16th century. In 1583 the Jesuit Thomas Stephens wrote from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> of likenesses between North Indian languages and Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, and in 1585 the merchant Filippo Sassetti compared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and Italian words, such as sapta and sette for seven. Neither led to further inquiry. In 1647 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> scholar Marcus Zuerius van Boxhorn proposed a common ancestor that he called Scythian.</p><p>In 1786 Sir William Jones told the Asiatic Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> bore a stronger affinity than could have arisen by accident and had sprung from some common source which perhaps no longer existed. The analysis of these relationships and the reconstruction of their common source were central to the development of historical linguistics as a discipline in the 19th century. The current consensus accepts no genetic link between the family and any other language family.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Indo-European languages” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its people spoke Lydian, an <b>Indo-European</b> language of the Anatolian family related to Hittite and Luwian, and Homer knew the region as Maeonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Many scholars identify them with speakers of the Tocharian languages, an early branch of <b>Indo-European</b> later attested in the Tarim Basin, and link them to the Tochari whom Greek writers name among the conquerors of Bactria; others are cautious, since no Yuezhi text survives.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Over its history Persian became one of the simplest <b>Indo-European languages</b> in its grammar.</blockquote><a href="/language/persian">Persian · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>The grammar of Sumerian is very different from that of the Semitic and <b>Indo-European languages</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/sumerian">Sumerian · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>Linguists use it for a branch of the <b>Indo-European languages</b>, archaeologists for the Hallstatt and La Tène cultures and their art.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Corded Ware culture may have been central to the spread of <b>Indo-European languages</b> in Europe.</blockquote><a href="/culture/corded-ware-culture">Corded Ware culture · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
